// -*- C++ -*-
//===----------------------------------------------------------------------===//
//
// std::hive implementation for the Bloomberg libc++ fork.
// P0447R28 as adopted into the C++26 working draft (2025-02 Hagenberg),
// including the post-R28 wording fixes applied by LWG before adoption
// (corrected deduction guides, is_within_hard_limits, "erroneous behavior"
// wording for out-of-limits hive_limits).
//
// Storage model: a doubly-linked list of fixed-capacity "groups". Each group
// owns four parallel allocations: the element block itself, a skipfield
// (jump-counting erased-run encoding used to skip over erased slots during
// iteration in O(1) amortized), and two free-list link arrays used to keep an
// O(1) doubly-linked list of erased runs ("skipblocks") per group, so that
// insertion can always find reusable storage without scanning.
//
// This deliberately differs from the reference implementation (plf::hive) in
// one respect: the free-list links there are written into the storage of the
// erased elements themselves (safe, since nothing else is live there) to
// avoid extra allocations. Here they live in their own __free_prev_/
// __free_next_ arrays instead. That costs extra memory per group (two more
// unsigned-sized arrays) but sidesteps needing sizeof(T) >= 2*sizeof(unsigned)
// and alignment reasoning for arbitrary T, which is worth the tradeoff for a
// from-scratch implementation. The skipfield encoding is also simplified
// relative to the classic "every node in a run stores its distance to the
// run's end" scheme: only the two boundary nodes of each erased run
// (its front and back) are kept accurate; interior nodes are left stale/
// unused. This is sufficient because iterators only ever *land* on a run's
// front (advancing into it) or back (retreating into it) -- interior
// positions are never read during normal traversal -- and it's what makes
// both traversal and erase-time run-merging O(1) regardless of run length:
// merging two runs only ever touches the (at most three) newly-relevant
// boundary positions, never a whole run's worth of nodes. Using the
// classic same-value-throughout encoding instead would make repeatedly
// erasing adjacent elements one at a time degrade to O(n) per call as the
// run grows, which is incompatible with the standard's per-call erase
// complexity ("linear in the number of elements erased" -- i.e. O(1) for a
// single-element erase).
//
// Not implemented, or implemented as an honest simplification: constexpr
// (the paper explicitly declines to require it, citing the same
// pervasive-reinterpret_cast concerns as here); the small-block-size /
// bitset-based storage variant for very small sizeof(T) (Appendix I of the
// paper) -- ordinary skipfield storage is used uniformly instead.
//
//===----------------------------------------------------------------------===//

